    {
      "id": "37j429t",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "SUBORDINATE COURTS"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Subordinate courts function at the district level and below, forming the primary interface between citizens and the judicial system.",
        "Article 233 regulates the appointment, posting, and promotion of District Judges by the Governor in consultation with the High Court.",
        "Article 234 provides for the recruitment of individuals other than district judges to the judicial service of a State.",
        "Article 235 places administrative, judicial, and disciplinary control of district and subordinate courts under the High Court.",
        "The lower judiciary is broadly divided into Civil Courts (headed by District Judge) and Criminal Courts (headed by Sessions Judge), alongside specialised forums like Revenue Courts, Family Courts, and Lok Adalats."
      ],
      "id": "ia9km7q"
    },
